1
00:00:12,494 --> 00:00:13,395
[SPEAKER_04]: Hello, Bhante.

2
00:00:13,455 --> 00:00:14,416
[SPEAKER_04]: My name is Zhiga.

3
00:00:14,796 --> 00:00:15,116
[SPEAKER_04]: Uh-huh.

4
00:00:15,637 --> 00:00:22,342
[SPEAKER_04]: And I think we can start with the subject of fear.

5
00:00:22,663 --> 00:00:36,454
[SPEAKER_04]: I think we can all agree that most of our everyday actions are, in some way, based in different ways of... different kinds of fears.

6
00:00:37,798 --> 00:00:43,421
[SPEAKER_04]: I often go to the to the root cause, to the origins of every fear.

7
00:00:44,481 --> 00:00:47,303
[SPEAKER_04]: And I think, this is my subject view,

8
00:00:48,255 --> 00:00:56,382
[SPEAKER_04]: That there should be one... one fundamental root cause of all fears we experience every day.

9
00:00:56,922 --> 00:01:09,572
[SPEAKER_04]: So, my question is, what is this, in your view, what is this... the root cause of those fears and how can we tackle it?

10
00:01:09,592 --> 00:01:12,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the root cause is death, fear of death and dying.

11
00:01:13,215 --> 00:01:16,658
[SPEAKER_01]: Every fear, I mean, it's... that's... that's where every fear is rooted.

12
00:01:17,541 --> 00:01:30,235
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, fear of losing possessions, losing loved ones, not getting what you want, it's all fearful because it implies certain end of your life, or at least end of your life the way you know it.

13
00:01:31,936 --> 00:01:38,503
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't tackle it in a sense of to not have it, because for as long as you're alive, you're liable to death.

14
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:44,113
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the ultimate contemplation the Buddha was talking about of maranusati.

15
00:01:44,533 --> 00:01:46,014
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't get rid of it.

16
00:01:46,174 --> 00:01:54,539
[SPEAKER_01]: The only thing you can do is develop your mind in regard to it through sīla, samādhi, and then through understanding.

17
00:01:55,119 --> 00:02:02,323
[SPEAKER_01]: But in order to do that, you pretty much need to stop ignoring the fact that you're gonna die.

18
00:02:04,004 --> 00:02:05,345
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when people act in their life,

19
00:02:06,495 --> 00:02:17,925
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's things, families, ambitions, every single of those actions is underlined by the... their mind ignoring their own mortality.

20
00:02:18,085 --> 00:02:25,592
[SPEAKER_01]: They never think that, I might not even live to see the end of this, because... because the life will become quite unpleasant.

21
00:02:26,741 --> 00:02:29,002
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's... that's an inevitable step.

22
00:02:29,602 --> 00:02:37,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless you admit the unpleasantness of life condemned to death, you cannot develop your mind towards death correctly, the way the Buddha was teaching.

23
00:02:38,765 --> 00:02:49,908
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the first step is to develop very strict virtue, sense restraint and keep the precepts, because otherwise the mind probably will not be able to bear it, if that's the direction you want to go and contemplate.

24
00:02:52,069 --> 00:02:53,950
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, thank you.

25
00:02:54,190 --> 00:02:54,890
[SPEAKER_06]: Then another... Sure.

26
00:02:55,372 --> 00:03:00,193
[SPEAKER_06]: You calm your mind when you're distressed by calming your mind before you get distressed.

27
00:03:00,253 --> 00:03:03,774
[SPEAKER_06]: So, don't wait till you're distressed to start calming your mind.

28
00:03:22,193 --> 00:03:30,659
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason your mind is not calm when you're distressed, is because you haven't cultivated calmness when you were not distressed.

29
00:03:30,699 --> 00:03:41,546
[SPEAKER_01]: So, before distress comes your way, and it will, one way or the other, eh... start restraining and not acting out of pressures, towards pleasure,

30
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:49,324
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's avoiding pain, endure that, and that's already preparing you for when distress or any other stress comes.

31
00:03:49,904 --> 00:03:54,027
[SPEAKER_01]: You will already have a ground for not resisting it blindly and spinning out of control.

32
00:03:58,589 --> 00:04:04,613
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is absolutely no samādhi, no calmness, without the basis of virtue and precepts.

33
00:04:04,693 --> 00:04:06,034
[SPEAKER_01]: It's absolutely not negotiable.

34
00:04:08,690 --> 00:04:17,894
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if a person doesn't keep precepts, or not so strictly, or not really committed to them, or sometimes yes, sometimes no, well, that's the type of samādhi you will have.

35
00:04:18,974 --> 00:04:23,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes you'll avoid this stress, sometimes you won't, but either way, you will be subjected to it.

36
00:04:26,767 --> 00:04:29,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Best way of dealing with distress when it happens.

37
00:04:29,428 --> 00:04:31,769
[SPEAKER_00]: So, suppose we were not following your advice.

38
00:04:32,289 --> 00:04:32,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, right.

39
00:04:32,989 --> 00:04:38,851
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you haven't followed the advice and now this distress happens and you will follow it in the future, but how to deal with the distress that has happened?

40
00:04:39,471 --> 00:04:39,911
[SPEAKER_01]: Endure it.

41
00:04:41,732 --> 00:04:43,913
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no magical, magical fix.

42
00:04:44,733 --> 00:04:49,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, if mind is not strong, the endurance will be unpleasant.

43
00:04:49,795 --> 00:04:52,296
[SPEAKER_01]: If mind is strong, the endurance can be pleasant.

44
00:04:52,316 --> 00:04:53,196
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter what it is.

45
00:04:54,233 --> 00:05:08,884
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like in the suttas, when the Buddha would be abused by people, shouted insults at him or something, and then some people noticed, look, that the Tathāgata, he's getting even brighter and happier when people do that to him, because it just reminds him how free he is.

46
00:05:09,205 --> 00:05:19,933
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if person is not developed and distress is there, make sure you don't do anything on account of that distress, that then later you have to deal with.

47
00:05:21,070 --> 00:05:37,166
[SPEAKER_01]: So, endure it, and even if it's like the advice from the suttas, if you grit your teeth, press the tongue against the roof of your mouth, and endure it until it passes, and then don't be careless when it disappears.

48
00:05:37,266 --> 00:05:38,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't forget that it will come back.

49
00:05:41,584 --> 00:05:43,105
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not gonna last forever, that's for sure.

50
00:05:43,125 --> 00:05:44,266
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter how bad it is.

51
00:05:44,966 --> 00:05:51,490
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind cannot possibly... it... the mind itself will run out of energy to... to... to spin out of control.

52
00:05:52,191 --> 00:05:58,575
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to wait till that happens and then start practicing and not be careless again.

53
00:05:58,595 --> 00:06:03,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Is this also part of the practice, enduring it and not getting distracted?

54
00:06:03,518 --> 00:06:03,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely.

55
00:06:04,179 --> 00:06:04,299
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

56
00:06:04,339 --> 00:06:06,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that is the core of the practice.

57
00:06:07,694 --> 00:06:17,423
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, enduring patiently is the only... it's the foremost and the only type of austerity, you know, endurance, that he approves of.

58
00:06:17,903 --> 00:06:21,786
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the austerity that can result, actually, in liberation.

59
00:06:21,826 --> 00:06:24,108
[SPEAKER_01]: So, people can endure all sorts of things.

60
00:06:24,268 --> 00:06:26,970
[SPEAKER_01]: People can endure dying out of hunger.

61
00:06:27,091 --> 00:06:30,854
[SPEAKER_01]: People can endure not breathing and whatever else those ascetics were doing.

62
00:06:31,094 --> 00:06:31,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Self-torture.

63
00:06:32,766 --> 00:06:38,052
[SPEAKER_01]: But people, what they cannot endure, is distress and fear on the mental level, without acting out of it.

64
00:06:38,592 --> 00:06:39,733
[SPEAKER_01]: For that, you need wisdom.

65
00:06:40,634 --> 00:06:42,216
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you do it, you will get wisdom.

66
00:06:43,818 --> 00:06:44,879
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it was the sutta...

67
00:06:45,139 --> 00:06:52,046
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't remember the exact number, but it was basically when this man was talking about types of austerity, which one that the targeter approves of.

68
00:06:52,988 --> 00:06:55,270
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said he'd done... he did all of them.

69
00:06:55,330 --> 00:07:05,961
[SPEAKER_01]: He went through... at one point he stopped breathing and had all this... like he said, basically, if there are physical pains to be experienced on account of enduring, he has experienced them and none of that was the right way.

70
00:07:05,981 --> 00:07:10,265
[SPEAKER_01]: And not acting out of... out of craving towards your own feelings.

71
00:07:10,305 --> 00:07:14,650
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, a person who can endure that, that is the foremost austerity.

72
00:07:15,837 --> 00:07:17,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else can match that.

73
00:07:17,497 --> 00:07:23,379
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if somebody, you know, I don't know, hangs from the top of a tree, never eats and never drinks water and dies like that or something.

74
00:07:23,999 --> 00:07:24,879
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not even close.

75
00:07:25,879 --> 00:07:33,781
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason why people do these extreme austerities, by the way, and, and, and endurances, is because they can't endure their own feelings without acting out of them.

76
00:07:34,201 --> 00:07:38,402
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's easier to double down and jump into it and, and try to deal with it on your own terms.

77
00:07:39,322 --> 00:07:41,923
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what ignorance is.

78
00:07:41,963 --> 00:07:42,843
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not in control.

79
00:07:43,678 --> 00:07:46,468
[SPEAKER_01]: And you need to start admitting that and feeling the weight of it.

80
00:07:51,457 --> 00:07:53,118
[SPEAKER_06]: So, related question.

81
00:07:53,578 --> 00:08:00,781
[SPEAKER_06]: How to practice sensory restraint in the presence of stimuli does not depend on your will.

82
00:08:00,821 --> 00:08:08,103
[SPEAKER_06]: Understand the restraint of the senses in the sense that, say, you don't eat or listen to music because of the pleasure.

83
00:08:08,864 --> 00:08:15,126
[SPEAKER_06]: What about when you weren't looking for a pleasant solution, but you still find yourself in it?

84
00:08:15,606 --> 00:08:19,748
[SPEAKER_06]: Say, do you eat at a restaurant when there's a good music?

85
00:08:20,528 --> 00:08:24,091
[SPEAKER_06]: When you drive past a very beautiful view, when someone hugs you.

86
00:08:24,751 --> 00:08:35,459
[SPEAKER_06]: I know that by retreating into solitude can minimize how often this happens, but it seems to me that this may fall more into realm of monastic life.

87
00:08:36,960 --> 00:08:40,823
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're in the world, the exposure to some of these things is inevitable.

88
00:08:41,403 --> 00:08:46,527
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you do is you have to realize that, well, it's already a compromise.

89
00:08:48,005 --> 00:08:53,601
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't expect for your mind to develop as much as it would if you learn how to deal with solitude.

90
00:08:56,922 --> 00:09:04,828
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're fine with that compromise, then just make sure to be very careful when you are in control of your circumstances.

91
00:09:04,908 --> 00:09:08,250
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you do have a say, make sure you don't slip up.

92
00:09:09,131 --> 00:09:13,674
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if accident... you know, if somebody hugs you or something, well, it won't last for three hours.

93
00:09:13,974 --> 00:09:16,156
[SPEAKER_01]: It's gonna be ten seconds and that's fine.

94
00:09:16,336 --> 00:09:23,061
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can endure it, because your mind has been sufficiently withdrawn from it when you were in charge of making these choices.

95
00:09:24,155 --> 00:09:44,742
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, if... when you are in charge of your sensory strength, if you... if... if you invest effort, proper effort, sufficient effort there, you won't worry as much about all these kind of accidental exposures, because the mind will not immediately leap up onto them, because it has already created some buffers onto the sensory strength that you practiced beforehand.

96
00:09:47,123 --> 00:09:47,903
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, eh...

97
00:09:49,258 --> 00:09:50,459
[SPEAKER_02]: Hello, my name is Sebastian.

98
00:09:51,019 --> 00:09:59,945
[SPEAKER_02]: I have a question about concrete suggestions for a lay follower.

99
00:10:00,065 --> 00:10:07,990
[SPEAKER_02]: So, for someone who has decided, for some reason, let's just assume at this point, that this is the fact of the matter, that

100
00:10:08,630 --> 00:10:11,191
[SPEAKER_02]: That person cannot ordain at that particular point.

101
00:10:11,732 --> 00:10:27,059
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what are some concrete suggestions, say, taking into account the vicissitudes of contemporary life as to how one could cultivate the virtuous and contemplative life as wisely and efficiently as possible.

102
00:10:27,159 --> 00:10:30,201
[SPEAKER_02]: For instance, how to engage with sexual restraint,

103
00:10:30,761 --> 00:10:35,965
[SPEAKER_02]: With limited food consumption, solitude, having contact with like-minded people, and so on and so forth.

104
00:10:36,305 --> 00:10:43,210
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what would be, say, some of your concrete suggestions for someone in a lay setting?

105
00:10:43,950 --> 00:10:55,518
[SPEAKER_02]: How could that person pursue these activities and disciplines as efficiently as possible, given the circumstances?

106
00:10:56,380 --> 00:10:58,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Some person can adjust it this way.

107
00:10:58,181 --> 00:10:59,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Some person can adjust the other way.

108
00:10:59,742 --> 00:11:11,111
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, the adjustment, whatever that might be, should be about minimizing your exposure to things that will tempt you and pressure you, where there is a good likelihood you will give in.

109
00:11:13,332 --> 00:11:15,774
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sell your TV.

110
00:11:18,536 --> 00:11:19,456
[SPEAKER_01]: Minimize your internet.

111
00:11:19,477 --> 00:11:21,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Only use it as a tool when you need it.

112
00:11:21,942 --> 00:11:26,245
[SPEAKER_01]: No browsing of the news, movies, series.

113
00:11:26,485 --> 00:11:29,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Use it for suttas and studies.

114
00:11:29,868 --> 00:11:33,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Minimize, like, unless you have to go to work where there are other people.

115
00:11:34,331 --> 00:11:38,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, when you don't have to go to work, don't go and socialize just for the sake of distraction.

116
00:11:40,215 --> 00:11:47,340
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, just read about the five precepts or eight precepts and see what you can do to support what those precepts require.

117
00:11:48,841 --> 00:11:51,123
[SPEAKER_01]: And then see where you fail and then improve there.

118
00:11:52,497 --> 00:12:12,678
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to... as I said in the... in my response to the earlier question, you have to recognize it is already a compromised situation, but... so you will have to do more of like a side work aimed at managing the circumstances, because they will tempt you and pressure you.

119
00:12:15,824 --> 00:12:17,965
[SPEAKER_02]: I would have an additional question here.

120
00:12:19,106 --> 00:12:24,449
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a very practical one and one that I was curious about for quite some time.

121
00:12:25,310 --> 00:12:36,097
[SPEAKER_02]: In certain Christian traditions, for instance, you would have lay movements that would imitate to a certain degree a monastic lifestyle.

122
00:12:36,497 --> 00:12:40,560
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this is something that has emerged in specific times.

123
00:12:41,060 --> 00:12:52,269
[SPEAKER_02]: I was just curious whether in the Theravāda tradition, there is something of the kind, whether there is, say, an institution of sorts, that is meant for lay people,

124
00:13:09,043 --> 00:13:19,312
[SPEAKER_02]: who would be interested in pursuing, say, the path of wisdom more intensively, without necessarily undergoing the full ordination.

125
00:13:20,283 --> 00:13:41,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, I think that's... that's what the Buddha encouraged to people back in the day that didn't... that couldn't ordain or that disrobed out of weakness or whatever else, to... to at least go back to the monastery and to post the days, keep the precepts, in other words, behave like monks, at least occasionally, because it's meritorious and it can help your mind and who knows, maybe it will spur another decision to become a monk again or something.

126
00:13:42,168 --> 00:13:43,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Today, I don't think there is a...

127
00:13:43,990 --> 00:13:49,414
[SPEAKER_01]: I think any monastery... just go and stay in any monastery and you will have to keep the eight precepts and kind of behave like monks.

128
00:13:49,555 --> 00:13:59,263
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, there are many Theravāda monasteries, retreat centers, where you can go and... gonna be... you have to be very restrained, follow the routine and so on.

129
00:13:59,363 --> 00:14:00,604
[SPEAKER_01]: So... so that's about it.

130
00:14:00,624 --> 00:14:00,824
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

131
00:14:01,605 --> 00:14:03,646
[SPEAKER_01]: You just go and become an anagarika into the monastery.

132
00:14:03,786 --> 00:14:05,568
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not a monk, but you have to behave like a monk.

133
00:14:09,018 --> 00:14:10,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Back in the West, we had to spend...

134
00:14:10,579 --> 00:14:22,641
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you go to Asia, and I think, in Thailand, you don't even have to be an anagarika, but in Europe, we had to be a whole year as an anagarika, then a whole year as a novice, and only then we get our upāsampada.

135
00:14:23,101 --> 00:14:30,103
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by that time, you'll kind of know if you really, you know, you know what to expect, and you know if you want to do it, and if you can do it.

136
00:14:31,243 --> 00:14:34,864
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for two years, you have to... and actually, some monasteries even longer, for two to three years

137
00:14:37,608 --> 00:14:37,868
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

138
00:14:40,650 --> 00:14:44,612
[SPEAKER_04]: I have several questions, but I will try to put it in one.

139
00:14:45,573 --> 00:14:50,456
[SPEAKER_04]: It's quite similar to the question before, for a lay person.

140
00:14:52,037 --> 00:15:04,425
[SPEAKER_04]: How... it is... which is the way to live in this society, which we, I think, can all agree, is based on wrong values?

141
00:15:04,965 --> 00:15:07,167
[SPEAKER_04]: How can we maintain our inner peace?

142
00:15:08,767 --> 00:15:25,340
[SPEAKER_04]: In other words, for example, in this society, you have to, most of the time, you have to struggle to maintain your ego, to protect this ego from bad influences, bad people, and so on.

143
00:15:26,201 --> 00:15:29,243
[SPEAKER_04]: And this can be sometimes very stressful.

144
00:15:29,303 --> 00:15:33,807
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what is your suggestion to keep inner peace?

145
00:15:34,707 --> 00:15:35,428
[SPEAKER_04]: Or try to?

146
00:15:36,818 --> 00:15:39,439
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have inner peace in that setting, as simple as that.

147
00:15:39,539 --> 00:15:44,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Household life is crowded and dusty, dirty, full of people with ignorance.

148
00:15:44,620 --> 00:15:45,980
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't have inner peace.

149
00:15:46,020 --> 00:15:49,041
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a reason that every sutta starts like that.

150
00:15:49,881 --> 00:15:52,902
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you truly want complete inner peace, you have to live that life.

151
00:15:54,082 --> 00:15:58,003
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you can't live that life, you have to recognize that you can only have a partial inner peace.

152
00:15:58,863 --> 00:16:01,304
[SPEAKER_01]: And that inner peace comes out of sense...

153
00:16:02,621 --> 00:16:03,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Out of self-control.

154
00:16:04,243 --> 00:16:06,945
[SPEAKER_01]: If you can develop self-control, and you can develop self-control.

155
00:16:06,985 --> 00:16:10,989
[SPEAKER_01]: There were lay people in the suttas that freed themselves from sensuality and ill-will.

156
00:16:11,149 --> 00:16:12,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Still in that setting.

157
00:16:12,590 --> 00:16:19,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, they were not as busy, as occupied, and as engaged as somebody who is completely uninterested in any development.

158
00:16:19,917 --> 00:16:23,120
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you do need to keep the precepts, protect your environment.

159
00:16:23,140 --> 00:16:24,622
[SPEAKER_01]: They would then protect your precepts.

160
00:16:24,682 --> 00:16:26,323
[SPEAKER_01]: They would then protect your sensory strength.

161
00:16:27,144 --> 00:16:28,645
[SPEAKER_01]: And start developing self-control.

162
00:16:28,805 --> 00:16:31,787
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're going to have to endure a lot of things, one way or the other.

163
00:16:31,807 --> 00:16:35,570
[SPEAKER_01]: As a monk, you have to endure a lot, but you want to stay there, you have to endure a lot.

164
00:16:35,730 --> 00:16:37,952
[SPEAKER_01]: As Yonaviru said, existence is a full-time job.

165
00:16:38,172 --> 00:16:41,714
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not just something people... like people like to think they just go through.

166
00:16:43,175 --> 00:16:50,901
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want inner peace, be clear with yourself how much of that peace you want, and how much you're willing to compromise on it.

167
00:16:52,023 --> 00:17:07,117
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah, precepts, five precepts, ideally eight precepts, and start developing that self-control through them, and then you will know exactly, you know, when to say no to people, and what's fine, what it isn't, because you start developing these criterias of your behavior.

168
00:17:08,017 --> 00:17:12,541
[SPEAKER_01]: But, probably the most important thing is to realize it's going to be hard work one way or the other.

169
00:17:12,702 --> 00:17:13,522
[SPEAKER_01]: It is unavoidable.

170
00:17:14,063 --> 00:17:16,025
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want peace, you have to work for it.

171
00:17:16,585 --> 00:17:18,367
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you work for it, the more peace you have.

172
00:17:19,729 --> 00:17:21,631
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what's, I think, Ajahn Chah.

173
00:17:21,971 --> 00:17:23,432
[SPEAKER_01]: You give up little, you get little piece.

174
00:17:23,673 --> 00:17:25,894
[SPEAKER_01]: You work to give up a lot, you get a lot of piece.

175
00:17:26,395 --> 00:17:29,758
[SPEAKER_01]: If you work and give everything up internally, well, you get all the piece.

176
00:17:31,419 --> 00:17:32,740
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't happen magically.

177
00:17:32,841 --> 00:17:36,243
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no magical technique, a recipe, a method.

178
00:17:36,984 --> 00:17:38,866
[SPEAKER_01]: They will then just do the work for you.

179
00:17:40,187 --> 00:17:42,108
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't be... you can't have it both ways.

180
00:17:42,308 --> 00:17:48,751
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't be in the world, engage with the world, and at the same time expect to be disengaged from the world and peaceful.

181
00:17:49,411 --> 00:17:50,032
[SPEAKER_01]: It's either or.

182
00:17:50,112 --> 00:17:50,732
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

183
00:17:50,832 --> 00:17:57,135
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a compromise and you have to see how far you yourself are willing to go in that compromise.

184
00:17:58,403 --> 00:18:06,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you really value the peace, you could already, you know, start tailoring your life and minimizing engagements.

185
00:18:06,105 --> 00:18:08,246
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be work, but then it will pay off later on.

186
00:18:08,366 --> 00:18:10,786
[SPEAKER_01]: You're gonna have less disturbances and so on.

187
00:18:10,966 --> 00:18:14,207
[SPEAKER_01]: If you can't do that, well, ask yourself, why can't you do that?

188
00:18:14,847 --> 00:18:15,528
[SPEAKER_01]: But what stops you?

189
00:18:17,048 --> 00:18:20,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, what stops anyone from leaving the household life?

190
00:18:24,450 --> 00:18:25,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Honestly speaking.

191
00:18:27,211 --> 00:18:28,051
[SPEAKER_01]: At any given second.

192
00:18:29,389 --> 00:18:29,769
[SPEAKER_01]: And same.

193
00:18:30,209 --> 00:18:31,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I say that to the monks.

194
00:18:32,190 --> 00:18:36,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, don't use the tradition and robes and the environment you live in to keep you a monk.

195
00:18:37,153 --> 00:18:43,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Make sure, basically, your mind prefers this lifestyle, so you go beyond any chance of this trouble on account of sensuality or anything stupid.

196
00:18:44,697 --> 00:18:52,420
[SPEAKER_01]: So, every time you should ask yourself, saying, what prevents me from just walking away from whatever I'm doing right now, this very second?

197
00:18:53,641 --> 00:18:55,342
[SPEAKER_01]: What sense of duty have I assumed?

198
00:18:57,983 --> 00:19:02,224
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to make sure you're clear about it, because that's how you will start to value the right things.

199
00:19:03,104 --> 00:19:04,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just do them out of sense.

200
00:19:04,365 --> 00:19:11,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, see, you might actually not be changing your environment sufficiently enough out of some blindly assumed sense of duty that you don't even really have.

201
00:19:11,947 --> 00:19:16,228
[SPEAKER_01]: But you just fear to think about it, so you don't give it up, so now you feel stuck.

202
00:19:17,508 --> 00:19:19,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I want peace, but I can't give this up.

203
00:19:19,229 --> 00:19:20,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can't have it both ways.

204
00:19:20,929 --> 00:19:24,290
[SPEAKER_01]: See which one you want more, and then grit your teeth and you have to endure it.

205
00:19:25,779 --> 00:19:29,501
[SPEAKER_01]: And then maybe, like, if you think solution will present itself later, fine.

206
00:19:29,861 --> 00:19:31,162
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to be patient until then.

207
00:19:31,242 --> 00:19:37,565
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, you have to be honest with yourself and see how much input you yourself have in your own environment.

208
00:19:38,725 --> 00:19:39,526
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not given.

209
00:19:39,826 --> 00:19:40,906
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say no to everything.

210
00:19:42,987 --> 00:19:44,328
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is a good reason to do so.

211
00:19:49,178 --> 00:19:57,546
[SPEAKER_06]: How to cultivate mindfulness or concentration to the extent that you are aware of your thinking and acting before you do it?

212
00:19:58,787 --> 00:20:02,330
[SPEAKER_06]: And how to recognize habits, thoughts that harm us along the way?

213
00:20:03,291 --> 00:20:08,456
[SPEAKER_06]: As I understand it, we should avoid the harmful, that's greed, resistance and delusions.

214
00:20:09,097 --> 00:20:12,120
[SPEAKER_06]: Both greed and resistance are fairly easy to detect.

215
00:20:12,380 --> 00:20:13,361
[SPEAKER_06]: What about delusions?

216
00:20:14,008 --> 00:20:16,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Delusions, practically, are distractions.

217
00:20:17,509 --> 00:20:20,971
[SPEAKER_01]: So, stuff you do to distract yourself through your senses.

218
00:20:21,551 --> 00:20:24,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Not necessarily sensual or not necessarily rooted in ill-will.

219
00:20:25,493 --> 00:20:31,615
[SPEAKER_01]: So, watching TV, reading news, talking to others, if there is no point.

220
00:20:31,835 --> 00:20:36,357
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why often the Buddha would talk a lot about avoiding idle chatter, pointless chatter and all.

221
00:20:36,577 --> 00:20:40,319
[SPEAKER_01]: It's even incorporated in those practical precepts.

222
00:20:40,739 --> 00:20:40,979
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

223
00:20:42,195 --> 00:20:49,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Things you would do throughout the day, little activities and so on, just to keep yourself busy, all of that would be cultivating delusion.

224
00:20:50,759 --> 00:20:57,062
[SPEAKER_01]: We covered that in that abiding in non-activity talk, I think, or at least part of it.

225
00:20:57,142 --> 00:20:59,883
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is, yeah, that is the hardest, obviously, to deal with.

226
00:21:00,724 --> 00:21:05,986
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can certainly start doing something about it on a small scale.

227
00:21:06,006 --> 00:21:11,569
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know, you're not central, you're not angry, but okay, so why don't you just sit with yourself doing nothing?

228
00:21:12,561 --> 00:21:17,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you need to, you know, potter around on your phone or do some work project or read something?

229
00:21:17,702 --> 00:21:18,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that necessary?

230
00:21:18,802 --> 00:21:21,703
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you depend on the activity of a neutral kind?

231
00:21:21,723 --> 00:21:28,545
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's it's not, as I said, unwholesome in terms of greed or real will, but you do depend on an external activity that requires your senses.

232
00:21:28,645 --> 00:21:36,326
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it is it is distracting in that sense and that's exactly why delusion is the hardest to see and hardest to uproot, as the Buddha would say.

233
00:21:37,567 --> 00:21:38,747
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I also have a question.

234
00:21:38,767 --> 00:21:38,827
[SPEAKER_03]: So,

235
00:21:40,601 --> 00:21:42,503
[SPEAKER_03]: So, we are all sort of on the way.

236
00:21:42,823 --> 00:21:46,807
[SPEAKER_03]: So, we all practice some sensory strength and probably some sīla.

237
00:21:47,247 --> 00:22:04,503
[SPEAKER_03]: Obviously, we are not, well, as also as lay people, but also probably also as monastics, that developed, that we can say, that we have accomplished already, for instance, the previous steps on the gradual training practice in jhāna.

238
00:22:05,023 --> 00:22:05,263
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah?

239
00:22:05,564 --> 00:22:07,005
[SPEAKER_03]: So, does it mean that

240
00:22:07,819 --> 00:22:15,045
[SPEAKER_03]: In order to start practicing jhāna, you need to, well, to accomplish all the previous steps.

241
00:22:15,585 --> 00:22:30,797
[SPEAKER_03]: Or is it like this, that you can start, sort of, a little bit of practice of jhāna, even if you are not completely sense-restrained and, yeah, maybe didn't fulfill some other steps completely?

242
00:22:31,397 --> 00:22:31,557
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

243
00:22:31,897 --> 00:22:32,578
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you have to.

244
00:22:32,738 --> 00:22:35,080
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to have fully developed in virtue.

245
00:22:36,430 --> 00:22:43,398
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they were even in the suttas where some monks did have jhānas and then they started slipping in their virtues and then they couldn't get jhānas anymore.

246
00:22:43,538 --> 00:22:45,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Although they knew exactly how and what to do.

247
00:22:46,221 --> 00:22:47,702
[SPEAKER_01]: But their actions undermined them.

248
00:22:48,543 --> 00:22:49,985
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's absolutely not negotiable.

249
00:22:50,085 --> 00:22:51,907
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, by the way, what jhāna is.

250
00:22:51,947 --> 00:22:56,312
[SPEAKER_01]: A full, thorough, complete withdrawal from unwholesome on the basis of

251
00:22:57,648 --> 00:23:10,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Your behavior that has been cultivated beforehand of withdrawal from the unwholesome, through keeping the precepts, abstaining from sexuality, ill-will, eh... pretty much all these things are rooted in greed and ill-will.

252
00:23:11,840 --> 00:23:14,041
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is already the practice of samādhi, by the way.

253
00:23:14,061 --> 00:23:16,403
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sīla, samādhi and then pañña.

254
00:23:17,404 --> 00:23:20,186
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not sīla and now let me go and find what samādhi is.

255
00:23:20,847 --> 00:23:24,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Perfecting your sīla is way of composing yourself rightly.

256
00:23:25,599 --> 00:23:27,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Away from the unwholesome states.

257
00:23:27,240 --> 00:23:32,564
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't have to think like you're not practicing jhāna simply by keeping precepts.

258
00:23:32,604 --> 00:23:33,585
[SPEAKER_01]: You are, actually.

259
00:23:34,185 --> 00:23:39,769
[SPEAKER_01]: You are closer to jhāna by keeping the precepts than if you're doing your ānāpānasati without the precepts.

260
00:23:42,111 --> 00:23:54,200
[SPEAKER_01]: It's withdrawing your mind from the unwholesome in the world, on account of which the pleasure from that withdrawal arises in the first jhāna, when the mind recognizes, I am safe here.

261
00:23:55,293 --> 00:24:04,884
[SPEAKER_01]: And the very first part of that withdrawal is the sīla and virtue, not a meditation technique or something you read you should do in order to get the jhāna.

262
00:24:09,409 --> 00:24:11,371
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, I understand this, but...

263
00:24:13,020 --> 00:24:23,305
[SPEAKER_03]: Nevertheless, should one still practice ānāpānasati or should one just completely give it up and, I don't know, just do nothing as you suggested in one of your talks?

264
00:24:23,465 --> 00:24:23,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, right.

265
00:24:23,946 --> 00:24:25,446
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it depends on your motivation.

266
00:24:25,626 --> 00:24:29,648
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're doing ānāpānasati because you can't sit still, you're not doing the right ānāpānasati.

267
00:24:29,668 --> 00:24:32,610
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just trying to find something to occupy yourself with.

268
00:24:33,950 --> 00:24:37,291
[SPEAKER_01]: You're being mindless about your own experience, not self-aware.

269
00:24:38,011 --> 00:24:45,033
[SPEAKER_01]: And many people would be doing meditation for that reason, because if they just sit aware, they start to lose their mind.

270
00:24:45,993 --> 00:24:52,154
[SPEAKER_01]: So meditation becomes something to keep you occupied while you're seemingly doing nothing, but you're actually doing a lot.

271
00:24:52,814 --> 00:24:53,995
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not ānāpānasati.

272
00:24:54,995 --> 00:24:59,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Ānāpānasati, as the Ānāpānasati Sutta says, well, it first requires the right view,

273
00:25:01,696 --> 00:25:05,879
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to have been very accomplished in virtue and understanding of the dhamma beforehand.

274
00:25:06,299 --> 00:25:10,241
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, secondly, it culminates in fulfillment of the four foundations of mindfulness.

275
00:25:11,722 --> 00:25:17,505
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no mention of ānāpānasati, boom, experience of jhāna, in the actual sutta of ānāpānasati that the Buddha was teaching.

276
00:25:19,597 --> 00:25:20,017
[SPEAKER_01]: So that...

277
00:25:20,297 --> 00:25:20,957
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not...

278
00:25:20,997 --> 00:25:27,539
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not trying to debate now what leads to jhāna or whatever, but that should be enough to just make you question your own view of what jhāna is.

279
00:25:28,079 --> 00:25:38,621
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't think we can get into that now, but if you... if you look into the suttas, you might see that it might be slightly different from what you're expecting it to be.

280
00:25:41,181 --> 00:25:43,702
[SPEAKER_01]: And the same reason, like, why do you want to practice jhāna?

281
00:25:43,802 --> 00:25:44,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, ask yourself.

282
00:25:46,527 --> 00:25:57,532
[SPEAKER_01]: While you know that maybe you haven't been really fulfilled in all the prerequisites, why do you ask, can I still... why do you want jhāna without having developed virtue and everything else?

283
00:25:57,572 --> 00:25:59,473
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you expect that jhāna to give to you?

284
00:26:01,433 --> 00:26:04,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you think it will bypass the fact that you haven't developed the virtue?

285
00:26:05,935 --> 00:26:10,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you think it will just give you the pleasure that it's nice, so you just want a bit of pleasure before you go back to work?

286
00:26:11,217 --> 00:26:12,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that is sensuality 101.

287
00:26:15,257 --> 00:26:19,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me go... let me get some pleasure, so I can then go back to work, so that I can get more pleasure.

288
00:26:20,621 --> 00:26:21,702
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not jhāna then.

289
00:26:25,425 --> 00:26:27,147
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just be clear about your motivation.

290
00:26:27,867 --> 00:26:34,432
[SPEAKER_01]: And while you're clarifying your motivation, why you want jhāna, why you want meditation and so on, what is... what is it supposed to accomplish for me?

291
00:26:35,413 --> 00:26:36,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Be clear about all those things.

292
00:26:36,934 --> 00:26:43,880
[SPEAKER_01]: While you're clarifying those things, keep practicing virtue and fulfilling those prerequisites that you know you should be fulfilling.

293
00:26:46,571 --> 00:26:51,573
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I can have a question that is somewhat related to what we were just talking about.

294
00:26:52,154 --> 00:27:03,298
[SPEAKER_02]: I would be very grateful if you could perhaps spend some time talking about the role of reflection and understanding in cultivating the right mindfulness.

295
00:27:03,479 --> 00:27:07,120
[SPEAKER_02]: I think this ties in nicely with what we've just been talking about.

296
00:27:07,620 --> 00:27:12,162
[SPEAKER_02]: Namely, there's this idea of... that is...

297
00:27:13,603 --> 00:27:26,331
[SPEAKER_02]: Very present and prominent in contemporary circles, whereby the idea is to cultivate mindfulness by merely observing, merely being aware.

298
00:27:27,032 --> 00:27:32,475
[SPEAKER_02]: So, developing your awareness that is devoid of all thought and understanding.

299
00:27:32,996 --> 00:27:42,502
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, the implicit idea is that you are somehow going to enter into a unique frame or state of mind that will somehow

300
00:27:43,918 --> 00:27:47,641
[SPEAKER_02]: ...absolve you of all the things that plague you, basically.

301
00:27:48,021 --> 00:27:57,908
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, I'm oversimplifying here intentionally, but I would say that the implicit premise behind many of the of the contemporary approaches is precisely this.

302
00:27:58,909 --> 00:28:08,416
[SPEAKER_02]: And whenever one reads suttas, one sees that verbs such as understanding and thinking and whatnot,

303
00:28:08,718 --> 00:28:15,482
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I would be very grateful if you could talk a little bit about the reflection, the role of reflection and understanding

304
00:28:35,015 --> 00:28:47,507
[SPEAKER_02]: In the contemplative path, maybe with concrete examples, so that it is more clear as to how one might integrate it as efficiently as possible into one's practice.

305
00:28:49,468 --> 00:29:06,714
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, before I answer that, we do have that whole playlist on our YouTube channel about meditation and a lot of those talks address exactly that, you know, the kind of contemporary notions of focusing practices and so on, where people try to avoid thinking in the problem, expecting it will magically disappear.

306
00:29:07,614 --> 00:29:11,296
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the idea of samādhi and jhāna become extension of that.

307
00:29:11,436 --> 00:29:18,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Just I'm focused hard enough on one-pointedness and then I have this special experience where I'm unaware of myself and everything has disappeared and was so blissful.

308
00:29:18,620 --> 00:29:23,563
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not just that that's not mindfulness, that's completely the opposite direction.

309
00:29:23,583 --> 00:29:25,464
[SPEAKER_01]: That, well, that's literally absence of your mind.

310
00:29:26,184 --> 00:29:29,306
[SPEAKER_01]: How can absence of your mind be fullness of your mind, mindful?

311
00:29:30,087 --> 00:29:31,587
[SPEAKER_01]: It's, it's just the opposite direction.

312
00:29:32,488 --> 00:29:34,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Reflection as, eh...

313
00:29:35,948 --> 00:29:42,209
[SPEAKER_01]: The role of reflection in the practice of the path, as you said, it's from the beginning till the end.

314
00:29:42,529 --> 00:29:44,109
[SPEAKER_01]: That's pretty much the unifying factor.

315
00:29:44,510 --> 00:29:49,590
[SPEAKER_01]: It is because of your reflection that you recognize the nature of your situation as a lay person.

316
00:29:50,431 --> 00:29:57,372
[SPEAKER_01]: It is because of your reflection that you recognize that there is an option to, you know, leave that, become a monk or at least get the precepts on.

317
00:29:57,752 --> 00:30:03,473
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it is because of your reflection that you adopt the moral set of behavior and the virtue and you start valuing it.

318
00:30:04,193 --> 00:30:07,134
[SPEAKER_01]: It is because of your reflection that you then grow out of your precepts.

319
00:30:07,534 --> 00:30:12,516
[SPEAKER_01]: It is because of your reflection that your mind experience calmness and composure on account of those precepts.

320
00:30:12,536 --> 00:30:13,356
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your samādhi.

321
00:30:14,016 --> 00:30:21,319
[SPEAKER_01]: And when that has developed, it is because of your reflection you get to understand the complete clarity, pretty much, of things that need to be understand for the freedom.

322
00:30:22,439 --> 00:30:25,640
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why Buddha said, you cannot have too much of mindfulness.

323
00:30:26,801 --> 00:30:28,801
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot have too much of that reflection.

324
00:30:30,022 --> 00:30:33,223
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sure, sometimes you can be more actively thinking about things,

325
00:30:33,965 --> 00:30:38,366
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, sometimes you need to calm down a bit, and just drop the subject, and calm down, and so on.

326
00:30:38,706 --> 00:30:42,587
[SPEAKER_01]: That's kind of the difference between vipassana and samatha practice.

327
00:30:43,147 --> 00:30:45,088
[SPEAKER_01]: But fundamentally, all is within mindfulness.

328
00:30:48,829 --> 00:30:52,550
[SPEAKER_01]: And the role is, is, well, immeasurable.

329
00:30:54,211 --> 00:31:01,253
[SPEAKER_01]: And often even, like again, when lay people will be talking to the Buddha, not even to the monks, he would... all he would do is encourage them to start, you know, questioning.

330
00:31:01,273 --> 00:31:05,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, don't go by the tradition, don't go by the, you know, seniority or something.

331
00:31:05,814 --> 00:31:07,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Ask yourself, wait, why do I value this?

332
00:31:07,754 --> 00:31:08,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I hold this?

333
00:31:08,534 --> 00:31:09,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I do this?

334
00:31:09,554 --> 00:31:10,595
[SPEAKER_01]: What do I want from my life?

335
00:31:11,075 --> 00:31:11,495
[SPEAKER_01]: And so on.

336
00:31:12,455 --> 00:31:15,716
[SPEAKER_01]: And the same... and that thing never changes, you know.

337
00:31:15,796 --> 00:31:22,077
[SPEAKER_01]: And then for a monk who has left that life and lives... he should keep constantly, hmm, is there any reason central lust in me?

338
00:31:22,925 --> 00:31:24,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there an arisen ill-will in me?

339
00:31:25,407 --> 00:31:27,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Can a non-arisen centralized arise in me?

340
00:31:27,989 --> 00:31:35,134
[SPEAKER_01]: No, he doesn't ask that there's some sort of, you know, pointless abstract questions of mantras that he repeats throughout the day, mumbles to himself.

341
00:31:35,175 --> 00:31:36,536
[SPEAKER_01]: No, he really needs to know.

342
00:31:37,296 --> 00:31:37,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Wait.

343
00:31:37,917 --> 00:31:40,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Can non-arisen unwholesome states arise in me?

344
00:31:42,220 --> 00:31:42,620
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

345
00:31:42,660 --> 00:31:43,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there you go.

346
00:31:44,822 --> 00:31:45,362
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your work.

347
00:31:45,402 --> 00:31:46,123
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to know.

348
00:31:46,503 --> 00:31:48,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Because not knowing means yes, they can.

349
00:31:49,677 --> 00:31:50,598
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not clear about it.

350
00:31:50,858 --> 00:31:52,139
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how those things arise.

351
00:31:52,499 --> 00:32:00,586
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, he diligently sets on to do the work and purifies his state... mind states, you know, first watch of the night, third watch of the night and so on.

352
00:32:00,926 --> 00:32:02,127
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what purification is.

353
00:32:02,167 --> 00:32:06,410
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not focusing on the nostril and becoming mindless, completely unaware of everything around you.

354
00:32:07,331 --> 00:32:18,580
[SPEAKER_01]: It's reviewing your mind, not allowing anything unwholesome to be welcomed, delighted in, accepted, not acting out of anything unwholesome and then contemplating your way out of it.

355
00:32:20,068 --> 00:32:23,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Through that, sense restraint and not acting out.

356
00:32:24,991 --> 00:32:27,652
[SPEAKER_01]: And none of that is possible to do if you're not mindful and reflexive.

357
00:32:32,035 --> 00:32:32,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I add something?

358
00:32:33,155 --> 00:32:35,757
[SPEAKER_02]: I would just like to... yeah, something.

359
00:32:38,091 --> 00:32:41,575
[SPEAKER_02]: I have a subsequent question that's related to this.

360
00:32:42,756 --> 00:32:57,414
[SPEAKER_02]: Why would you say thoughts and thinking in general nowadays has become so demonized to the point where Buddhism, for many people, is basically identified with non-thinking?

361
00:32:58,273 --> 00:33:13,366
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this ideal, or this idea of thinking being the main culprit of all the existential problems, is this maybe, you know, a symptom of...

362
00:33:14,411 --> 00:33:36,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I don't know specifically the Western mind, but basically it's a symptom of a mind or a symptom of a culture that has proliferated significantly into sensuality.

363
00:33:37,700 --> 00:33:44,523
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the expectation for quick fix and blaming the external things for problems, it's the automatic default behavior.

364
00:33:46,003 --> 00:33:52,826
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when sometimes people cannot blame the obvious external things for their problems, well, the next external thing is my thinking in regard to the problem.

365
00:33:53,406 --> 00:34:04,270
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then they turn to spirituality, hopefully, and spirituality does offer that, because, you know, most of those people believe in the same solutions, as in stop thinking and the problem will disappear.

366
00:34:05,390 --> 00:34:06,991
[SPEAKER_01]: As like, well, thinking was never the problem.

367
00:34:08,156 --> 00:34:08,917
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the whole point.

368
00:34:08,997 --> 00:34:15,706
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as you think, ironically enough, that your thinking is the problem, well, you're contradicting yourself.

369
00:34:16,467 --> 00:34:19,731
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't stop thinking, because trying to stop thinking is your own thought.

370
00:34:20,677 --> 00:34:26,481
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all you're hoping is, basically, hopefully, sufficiently enough, I can ignore this problem, and then it will go away.

371
00:34:26,941 --> 00:34:27,682
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what people...

372
00:34:27,702 --> 00:34:28,182
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

373
00:34:28,202 --> 00:34:33,786
[SPEAKER_01]: They expect a quick fix, and the quickest fix is block your thoughts from recognizing the problem.

374
00:34:33,926 --> 00:34:42,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, hey, my thoughts reveal that problem is there, let me then deal with the problem, then, regardless of what I think, there will be no problem there.

375
00:34:42,292 --> 00:34:43,473
[SPEAKER_01]: That's too much work now.

376
00:34:44,744 --> 00:34:46,105
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's sensuality.

377
00:34:46,186 --> 00:34:47,307
[SPEAKER_01]: It's sensual behavior.

378
00:34:47,327 --> 00:34:50,130
[SPEAKER_01]: And I don't mean like crazy indulgent or something.

379
00:34:50,150 --> 00:35:07,788
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just people depend on their senses, things that come through their senses, activities, distractions, and which means every time you engage in that direction, you are losing basis for developing patience, basis for developing endurance, and basis for developing self-reflection.

380
00:35:09,271 --> 00:35:12,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Because this is pleasant and covers any problem up.

381
00:35:12,673 --> 00:35:20,957
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why do I need to bother to dig up a wound and feel the pain of it, where I can just paste this magical cream and feel nothing and continue with my life?

382
00:35:21,537 --> 00:35:24,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, until you can't and then it's too late to do anything about it.

383
00:35:24,818 --> 00:35:27,900
[SPEAKER_01]: The wound has festered and spread around and now you're done.

384
00:35:29,201 --> 00:35:31,822
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, the sensuality will prevent you to see that.

385
00:35:32,182 --> 00:35:34,023
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why people don't see the peril in it.

386
00:35:36,311 --> 00:35:45,434
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, blaming thoughts and that's why, exactly, as I said, meditation practices that are about focusing and not thinking, are exactly that.

387
00:35:46,314 --> 00:35:52,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Extension of sensuality, like an animal trying to hunt a prey that it really values and it will provide me with satisfaction.

388
00:35:53,096 --> 00:36:00,819
[SPEAKER_01]: So, people are trying to hunt the special experience, they will now provide me with great pleasure and all the disappearances of my problems.

389
00:36:01,519 --> 00:36:01,879
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

390
00:36:02,059 --> 00:36:05,160
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what people try, that's what most people turn to religion for.

391
00:36:06,348 --> 00:36:15,315
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's fine if you turn to the religion for those mistaken reasons, but it's not fine if you never actually start questioning those and hopefully, you know, upgrade your expectations and practice.

392
00:36:16,997 --> 00:36:21,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, central mind cannot expect enlightenment to be anything other than great sensuality.

393
00:36:22,281 --> 00:36:22,822
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's fine.

394
00:36:23,722 --> 00:36:30,328
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's when you start to do it and learn about it, if you never abandon those sensual expectations, well, then you're just... well, you're not getting anywhere.

395
00:36:32,488 --> 00:36:37,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you were just talking about the reflection and contemplation practices.

396
00:36:37,932 --> 00:36:40,915
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how to get to the root of the problem?

397
00:36:40,995 --> 00:36:42,456
[SPEAKER_00]: How to...

398
00:36:42,556 --> 00:36:45,098
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I was just thinking about this.

399
00:36:45,458 --> 00:36:48,241
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize that you have a problem, right?

400
00:36:48,941 --> 00:36:56,267
[SPEAKER_00]: And would you say that the correct way of contemplating and meditating upon that problem

401
00:36:58,048 --> 00:37:02,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Why a certain thing causes desire, aversion, etc.

402
00:37:02,332 --> 00:37:10,761
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the why, but the breaking it down into the constituent parts, into the five khandhas, for example.

403
00:37:13,564 --> 00:37:14,325
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

404
00:37:14,365 --> 00:37:19,851
[SPEAKER_01]: That would be basically pretty much an abstract analysis you're doing in order to get rid of the problem.

405
00:37:22,100 --> 00:37:27,402
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you see... if you wanna... if you see a problem of five khandhas and so on, you wanna see that from the problem.

406
00:37:27,562 --> 00:37:30,223
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't wanna be putting it into a problem and making it be so.

407
00:37:30,823 --> 00:37:34,944
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what often people... oh, I reflect this and everything is anicca and when anicca comes, I look at this.

408
00:37:35,044 --> 00:37:41,947
[SPEAKER_01]: All that is just trying to manage this problem that you can't deal with using Buddhist terminology and so on.

409
00:37:42,667 --> 00:37:47,809
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to recognize that whatever problem is there, first of all, it doesn't matter why.

410
00:37:49,087 --> 00:37:50,107
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter how.

411
00:37:50,287 --> 00:37:51,268
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter who.

412
00:37:51,888 --> 00:37:58,590
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the simile the Buddha gave in the suttas, like a man gets shot with an arrow and he's bleeding and it's very painful.

413
00:37:59,110 --> 00:38:07,673
[SPEAKER_01]: But he refuses to deal with bleeding and the pain until he finds out who shot him, why they shot him, what clan was the person that shot him.

414
00:38:08,726 --> 00:38:11,751
[SPEAKER_01]: Could have he done something to... to not be... All of that is...

415
00:38:11,791 --> 00:38:13,514
[SPEAKER_01]: He wastes his time and he bleeds to death.

416
00:38:13,874 --> 00:38:21,727
[SPEAKER_01]: He tries to manage his suffering, provide the satisfying answers to justified suffering, all in order to not deal with the suffering.

417
00:38:22,614 --> 00:38:33,837
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the first step to recognize is that doesn't matter why, what, what for and who, matters is that sooner or later something will bother you, something will arise for you and that's the problem.

418
00:38:34,237 --> 00:38:36,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter where it came from or for what reason.

419
00:38:36,977 --> 00:38:43,019
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you start and that's already the first step to stop blaming the world or trying to justify or psychologize.

420
00:38:43,539 --> 00:38:45,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is what happened, so next time I won't do that.

421
00:38:46,079 --> 00:38:48,980
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is to try to prevent your liability to suffer.

422
00:38:49,400 --> 00:38:52,521
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way you can prevent that is by becoming fully enlightened person.

423
00:38:53,502 --> 00:39:22,064
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize it's not about management, it's not about finding the underlying causes or the reasons, it's about accepting the liability to suffering, feeling the suffering here and now, whenever it wants to arise, not trying to prolong it, but not trying to get rid of it either, enduring it, not breaking the precepts on account of it, not distracting yourself with sensuality on account of it, and that will already force your mind to see it for what it is, or at least stop turning a blind eye to it.

424
00:39:23,607 --> 00:39:38,351
[SPEAKER_01]: And from there, you will get to... if you endure that, that pure dukkha, without a cover-up, without psychologizing, without all these distractions in regard to the dukkha, well, everything else you read in the suttas, we're gonna start to make more sense.

425
00:39:39,031 --> 00:39:42,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now you actually have a basis for the things that the Buddha was talking about.

426
00:39:43,735 --> 00:40:00,609
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how your your contemplation on dhamma and so on, from that abstract level of, you know, this is one aggregate, two aggregates, three aggregates, comes down onto the very concrete level of you see it in what you're enduring, not at the expense of and not in regard to get rid of what bothers you.

427
00:40:01,569 --> 00:40:10,136
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, incidentally enough, that will get rid of all the suffering, full understanding, but not through the management, but through what we say uprooting in our talks.

428
00:40:11,197 --> 00:40:12,158
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the big difference.

429
00:40:14,715 --> 00:40:23,760
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might... your mind might start picking up stuff that you read from the suttas, but make sure you're not motivated by trying to get rid of the suffering.

430
00:40:24,180 --> 00:40:25,341
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not trying to understand it.

431
00:40:25,401 --> 00:40:29,983
[SPEAKER_01]: You're trying to get rid of it with justifications, using the information you collected from the suttas.

432
00:40:31,742 --> 00:40:34,844
[SPEAKER_01]: And many people do that, like, oh, yeah, yeah, everything is anicca.

433
00:40:34,964 --> 00:40:36,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I understand that.

434
00:40:36,044 --> 00:40:40,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Especially when things bother them, they keep repeating the mantra, everything is anicca, everything is anicca.

435
00:40:40,667 --> 00:40:43,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because they understood anicca, or they see directly.

436
00:40:43,488 --> 00:40:46,730
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the mantra helps them dismiss the pain.

437
00:40:48,231 --> 00:40:50,512
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not the anicca the Buddha was teaching about.

438
00:40:51,372 --> 00:40:53,834
[SPEAKER_01]: You first need to endure things in order to see their anicca.

439
00:40:54,474 --> 00:40:57,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Not apply and paste, like, your response over them.

440
00:41:01,199 --> 00:41:01,800
[SPEAKER_00]: I think you said...

441
00:41:02,020 --> 00:41:04,222
[SPEAKER_00]: I think you said what I was trying to say, actually.

442
00:41:05,983 --> 00:41:12,829
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not... No, I didn't mean the abstract, breaking it down, but feeling the feeling... Well, sure.

443
00:41:12,989 --> 00:41:25,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, again, maybe I used abstract in too concise way, but the point is, if you're trying to even contemplate these aggregates and everything else that you read from the suttas, in order to deal with the pain,

444
00:41:26,656 --> 00:41:28,057
[SPEAKER_01]: means you want to get rid of your pain.

445
00:41:28,077 --> 00:41:30,178
[SPEAKER_01]: You're acting out of aversion towards pain.

446
00:41:30,298 --> 00:41:31,859
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you will not see the dhamma.

447
00:41:32,480 --> 00:41:34,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you haven't read the right sutta.

448
00:41:34,141 --> 00:41:38,604
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because you still cultivate the basic principle of craving that the dhamma begins with.

449
00:41:39,624 --> 00:41:45,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Aversion towards pain, lusting after pleasure, and ignoring the neutral feeling.

450
00:41:46,768 --> 00:41:59,482
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you will inevitably have to start like that, but if you keep your... that's what I mean, if you keep your mind on motivation, why am I doing this, it can become apparent to you, that you are trying to contemplate the dhamma, because you're still cultivating aversion towards pain.

451
00:42:00,703 --> 00:42:07,230
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you cannot get to the dhamma, because of that aversion, not because of your insufficient cultivation or contemplation.

452
00:42:10,983 --> 00:42:12,924
[SPEAKER_01]: So, suffering needs to be accepted.

453
00:42:13,305 --> 00:42:16,907
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Buddha said, I teach only two things, suffering and freedom from it.

454
00:42:18,248 --> 00:42:22,511
[SPEAKER_01]: So, most people cannot even suffer, because they immediately go into the mode of aversion.

455
00:42:22,751 --> 00:42:28,635
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever they do, even practice, become monks or whatever, is to get rid of the suffering through aversion.

456
00:42:30,236 --> 00:42:33,739
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as a ultimate goal, you... sure, you want to be free from suffering.

457
00:42:33,979 --> 00:42:34,439
[SPEAKER_01]: That's fine.

458
00:42:35,880 --> 00:42:38,442
[SPEAKER_01]: But how you go about it, will determine whether you succeed or not.

459
00:42:39,548 --> 00:42:43,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Is your practice of getting rid still rooted in aversion towards that suffering?

460
00:42:44,310 --> 00:42:53,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are you realizing, okay, I want to get rid, which means now I have to endure suffering for what it is, not try to remove it on my own terms.

461
00:42:54,714 --> 00:42:59,976
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you will basically stop acting out of aversion towards suffering.

462
00:43:00,056 --> 00:43:03,257
[SPEAKER_03]: Can you please clarify what you mean on your own terms?

463
00:43:04,430 --> 00:43:05,470
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, that's what it is.

464
00:43:05,490 --> 00:43:08,972
[SPEAKER_01]: See, like, there is suffering present, for example, something bothers you.

465
00:43:10,172 --> 00:43:12,773
[SPEAKER_01]: You already, by default, refuse to accept it.

466
00:43:13,414 --> 00:43:18,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you say, I accept this suffering, emotionally, you are averse towards it, as the suttas would say.

467
00:43:18,776 --> 00:43:20,236
[SPEAKER_01]: There is aversion there, enduring.

468
00:43:21,017 --> 00:43:33,522
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever you do now, it's rooted in you acting out of that aversion, which means, whatever you do will be, basically, things you want to do in order to remove this, which is your averse, but you're not removing your aversion.

469
00:43:34,627 --> 00:43:47,495
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's like, you try to do it on your own terms, as opposed to realizing that you must accept the suffering, not try to do anything rooted in aversion, not try to do anything rooted on... in your own terms, and then endure it.

470
00:43:47,835 --> 00:43:51,497
[SPEAKER_01]: See, enduring it is not on your own terms, because you don't want to do it.

471
00:43:51,517 --> 00:43:52,378
[SPEAKER_01]: You're averse to it.

472
00:43:53,620 --> 00:43:58,264
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you endured it sufficiently, without breaking the precepts or acting out of it, that's what I mean.

473
00:43:58,584 --> 00:44:02,787
[SPEAKER_01]: Stuff you read from the suttas, you start seeing it, you start recognizing it in it.

474
00:44:03,268 --> 00:44:05,930
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to be applying it and thinking it actively.

475
00:44:06,570 --> 00:44:10,313
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, oh, as the suttas would say, it would become apparent.

476
00:44:10,834 --> 00:44:11,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, look, it's there.

477
00:44:11,975 --> 00:44:15,838
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason I wasn't seeing it, because I was too busy acting out of aversion to see it.

478
00:44:17,934 --> 00:44:24,759
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the difference between doing it on your own terms, or enduring it first, which will prevent you to act out on your own terms.

479
00:44:31,365 --> 00:44:33,046
[SPEAKER_06]: So, Krishan, from...

480
00:44:36,168 --> 00:44:36,729
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay.

481
00:44:37,249 --> 00:44:46,296
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I am... reflected now a little bit on your... on your answer to my question about samādhi and jhāna.

482
00:44:46,717 --> 00:44:46,957
[SPEAKER_03]: So,

483
00:44:48,453 --> 00:45:06,671
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I mean, what if, for an individual, the inability to experience that peace that one probably experiences at retreats, leads him to abandon the lay life and become a monastic?

484
00:45:07,011 --> 00:45:07,912
[SPEAKER_03]: So, is it...

485
00:45:09,243 --> 00:45:19,626
[SPEAKER_03]: It seems to me that this experience of samādhi, even if it is connected with sensuality, can be beneficial for the progress.

486
00:45:20,206 --> 00:45:22,327
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you view it?

487
00:45:22,867 --> 00:45:27,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, becoming a monk is not a progress in itself.

488
00:45:27,689 --> 00:45:30,389
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you have a wrong view, it won't change.

489
00:45:30,829 --> 00:45:32,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you wear robes, it doesn't matter.

490
00:45:33,868 --> 00:45:37,009
[SPEAKER_01]: So, progress is if you experience the right peace.

491
00:45:37,530 --> 00:45:39,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Progress is if you're clear from the wrong views.

492
00:45:40,111 --> 00:45:43,772
[SPEAKER_03]: But this desire for peace... Yeah.

493
00:45:43,912 --> 00:45:46,974
[SPEAKER_01]: So, see, the problem with that is desire for peace is fine.

494
00:45:48,855 --> 00:45:52,096
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's fine if you're going to retreat and get the best peace you can.

495
00:45:53,156 --> 00:46:01,200
[SPEAKER_01]: But what is not fine is to think, well, this peace stands in place of the right peace, because then you will not seek what the right peace is.

496
00:46:02,438 --> 00:46:04,480
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's my entire point here.

497
00:46:04,660 --> 00:46:06,182
[SPEAKER_01]: You should keep trying to get the peace.

498
00:46:06,983 --> 00:46:07,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody says you shouldn't.

499
00:46:08,544 --> 00:46:19,695
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you must be very careful is, when you do so, you might fall into a view that the relative peace you find, since that's the only peace you know, will be conflated with the peace that the Buddha was teaching.

500
00:46:19,816 --> 00:46:20,716
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not the case.

501
00:46:20,817 --> 00:46:24,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Because otherwise, you would be at least a sotāpanna, anāgāmī or an arahant.

502
00:46:25,293 --> 00:46:29,176
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's always my point when I say, all the Bhante meditation techniques help me.

503
00:46:29,597 --> 00:46:34,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, are you confusing that help for the, like, fulfillment of the dhamma?

504
00:46:34,881 --> 00:46:44,789
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are you fully aware that that help is just managing until I find a way how to pull the arrow out, but I never forget that it's pulling of the arrow out that my goal is?

505
00:46:46,231 --> 00:46:47,472
[SPEAKER_01]: And people often do forget that.

506
00:46:47,832 --> 00:46:53,737
[SPEAKER_01]: If you manage your pain, if you find that temporary peace long enough, you will start believing in it.

507
00:46:55,223 --> 00:46:56,564
[SPEAKER_01]: So, don't let that happen to you.

508
00:46:57,104 --> 00:47:11,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, now you might be aware, but if you do start making the effort, or rather, the more effort you make towards management, the more risk there is, you're gonna start believing that is what the practice is, and because of that, you won't start pushing further to find what the actual practice is.

509
00:47:12,552 --> 00:47:17,095
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what happens when people go to retreats and have super special peaceful experiences.

510
00:47:17,175 --> 00:47:19,436
[SPEAKER_01]: They're just too pleasing to be given up now.

511
00:47:20,454 --> 00:47:23,397
[SPEAKER_01]: And somebody comes and tells them, no, no, that's not the peace.

512
00:47:23,417 --> 00:47:25,258
[SPEAKER_01]: The peace of understanding is completely different.

513
00:47:25,699 --> 00:47:27,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, they'll get upset with that person.

514
00:47:28,661 --> 00:47:30,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of the pleasure of the peace.

515
00:47:30,263 --> 00:47:33,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because they fully understood what that peace was and they know this person is wrong.

516
00:47:33,806 --> 00:47:33,946
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

517
00:47:34,086 --> 00:47:36,628
[SPEAKER_01]: It's simply threat to the pleasure that they know.

518
00:47:36,868 --> 00:47:37,229
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

519
00:47:37,589 --> 00:47:39,651
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all it takes for you to fall into the wrong view.

520
00:47:41,042 --> 00:47:44,385
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're careful, if you're transparent about it, fine, no problem.

521
00:47:45,006 --> 00:47:45,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Keep trying.

522
00:47:45,746 --> 00:47:53,173
[SPEAKER_01]: At any peace you can get, just don't lose perspective that peace begins only with Sotāpatti or higher, not with a special experience of your meditation.

523
00:47:57,597 --> 00:47:58,337
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, okay, yeah.

524
00:47:58,457 --> 00:48:02,541
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what exactly what my point was and also what my understanding is.

525
00:48:02,581 --> 00:48:03,922
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, thank you.

526
00:48:04,263 --> 00:48:06,645
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if possible, I have another question.

527
00:48:07,185 --> 00:48:07,445
[SPEAKER_03]: So,

528
00:48:08,500 --> 00:48:28,816
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so we are discussing now very practical things and, yeah, and this is, yeah, this is very helpful for me, but, so, but there are some things in Buddhism which I have a real problem with, like, for instance, like belief in rebirth and things like this and maybe some talks about cosmology that Buddha gave.

529
00:48:29,336 --> 00:48:31,638
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what is the attitude

530
00:48:33,579 --> 00:48:37,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't conflate the two.

531
00:48:37,261 --> 00:48:43,763
[SPEAKER_01]: You might have a problem with those side things, and who knows where those conversations were rooted in, and what those things meant.

532
00:48:43,823 --> 00:48:46,664
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, like in the sūtra it says 500 arahants came, and so on.

533
00:48:47,304 --> 00:48:53,186
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just 500 meant... it was like a... like a... a common... common way of saying many.

534
00:48:53,206 --> 00:48:53,706
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not...

535
00:48:53,726 --> 00:48:56,307
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not saying that, oh, the Buddha didn't mean the...

536
00:48:57,806 --> 00:49:00,727
[SPEAKER_01]: So, take that as something that maybe you can think about later.

537
00:49:25,478 --> 00:49:32,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't look for faults on account of which you will avoid doing things you are capable of and you would benefit immensely from.

538
00:49:35,232 --> 00:49:36,833
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, you don't need to accept it, by the way.

539
00:49:36,893 --> 00:49:37,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you must believe.

540
00:49:37,673 --> 00:49:38,114
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you don't.

541
00:49:38,514 --> 00:49:43,337
[SPEAKER_01]: It's never directly related to any relevant sutta, freedom from suffering or anything else.

542
00:49:43,917 --> 00:49:46,159
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize, yeah, it's not really directly related.

543
00:49:46,899 --> 00:49:50,901
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm sure there were plenty of people in India at the time, they didn't believe in rebirth.

544
00:49:51,222 --> 00:49:53,843
[SPEAKER_01]: They have all sorts of teachers who said, no, this is the only life and so on.

545
00:49:54,704 --> 00:49:56,745
[SPEAKER_01]: And it was not a requirement.

546
00:49:56,905 --> 00:49:57,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Because why?

547
00:49:57,606 --> 00:49:58,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you suffer in this life.

548
00:49:59,006 --> 00:50:00,687
[SPEAKER_01]: You're pressured by your emotions in this life.

549
00:50:00,988 --> 00:50:03,249
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't escape from fear and anxiety in this life.

550
00:50:03,809 --> 00:50:03,949
[SPEAKER_01]: That

551
00:50:08,701 --> 00:50:14,282
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, accepting Buddhist cosmology does not make you more of a Buddhist than if you don't accept it.

552
00:50:14,842 --> 00:50:23,904
[SPEAKER_01]: What makes you more of a Buddhist is if you keep the five precepts, the eight precepts, and apply, basically, your tireless energy day and night to cultivate the Buddhist instruction.

553
00:50:24,564 --> 00:50:24,924
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

554
00:50:25,004 --> 00:50:26,164
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what makes you Buddhist.

555
00:50:26,884 --> 00:50:32,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Not your proclamational belief in, you know, gods and so on, that the Buddha laid out in those suttas.

556
00:50:34,006 --> 00:50:35,106
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it bothers you, well,

557
00:50:36,052 --> 00:50:42,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't let that become a motivation for avoiding things that you can't practice in this life, that you can't understand for yourself in this life.

558
00:50:42,834 --> 00:50:44,014
[SPEAKER_01]: So, endure that bother.

559
00:50:44,454 --> 00:50:45,554
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, that's what I'm saying.

560
00:50:48,775 --> 00:51:01,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Endurance is pretty much the crossroads people come at between either you're going to manage, try to manage and get rid of your suffering, or you're actually going the right way of being able to approach it eventually and get the right view and so on.

561
00:51:03,126 --> 00:51:04,407
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not blind endurance.

562
00:51:04,467 --> 00:51:06,508
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not focus on your nostrils and endure.

563
00:51:06,849 --> 00:51:13,493
[SPEAKER_01]: It's mindful, full self-awareness, not acting out of greed, aversion or distraction endurance.

564
00:51:13,513 --> 00:51:15,495
[SPEAKER_01]: The foremost austerity that the Buddha talks about.

565
00:51:17,256 --> 00:51:18,057
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it is.

566
00:51:18,177 --> 00:51:19,238
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like the basis.

567
00:51:20,478 --> 00:51:23,400
[SPEAKER_01]: The basis for the right practice, for the direction of the right practice.

568
00:51:23,701 --> 00:51:25,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Being able to endure things without acting out.

569
00:51:28,504 --> 00:51:31,626
[SPEAKER_01]: And you probably hear or read me often saying that.

570
00:51:31,666 --> 00:51:32,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Not act out.

571
00:51:33,751 --> 00:51:42,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Not act out rooted in emotions against which you have either aversion or lust or you want to distract yourself from.

572
00:51:42,478 --> 00:51:53,947
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sīla and sensory strain needs to come first as a container, because doesn't matter how much mentally you wish to not act out, if your body still carries pretty much the habit of acting out, you are acting out.

573
00:51:54,287 --> 00:51:57,550
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just wishful thinking to believe, you know.

574
00:52:00,790 --> 00:52:00,890
[SPEAKER_06]: Okay.

575
00:52:00,910 --> 00:52:01,931
[SPEAKER_06]: So, Sebastian has a question.

576
00:52:02,291 --> 00:52:05,152
[SPEAKER_02]: I have a question about anxiety.

577
00:52:05,252 --> 00:52:06,752
[SPEAKER_02]: We've touched upon this briefly.

578
00:52:06,912 --> 00:52:14,035
[SPEAKER_02]: I would like to kind of bring forth this, what seems to be like a dual aspect of anxiety.

579
00:52:15,115 --> 00:52:22,658
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, on the one hand, of course, it seems to be an unwholesome mental state.

580
00:52:23,839 --> 00:52:26,540
[SPEAKER_02]: But on the other hand, there's something to be said

581
00:52:27,480 --> 00:52:33,685
[SPEAKER_02]: for a potentially beneficial impact and aspect of anxiety.

582
00:52:34,345 --> 00:52:40,290
[SPEAKER_02]: After all, it was existential anxiety that led Buddha on his path.

583
00:52:41,671 --> 00:52:46,415
[SPEAKER_02]: It is existential anxiety that Buddha speaks of when he says that monks everything is burning.

584
00:52:47,055 --> 00:52:54,421
[SPEAKER_02]: It is existential anxiety that you said yourself is sometimes lacking even in among the monastics.

585
00:52:55,061 --> 00:52:55,962
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how does one

586
00:52:56,604 --> 00:53:06,288
[SPEAKER_02]: Cultivate this existential anxiety, existential dread, this ongoing awareness of, let's say, mortality, in the broadest sense of the term.

587
00:53:06,468 --> 00:53:10,550
[SPEAKER_01]: With anxiety, basically, the problem with anxiety is that people want to get rid of it because it's unpleasant.

588
00:53:11,691 --> 00:53:13,632
[SPEAKER_01]: In itself, anxiety is not unwholesome.

589
00:53:13,752 --> 00:53:21,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Anxiety is just basically revealing that there is something that can still undermine you, that you can still suffer on account of.

590
00:53:22,035 --> 00:53:23,516
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anxiety is a mental state.

591
00:53:23,857 --> 00:53:30,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, people like to think it's unwholesome because it justifies them trying to get rid of it by any means necessary, you know, including medications and so on.

592
00:53:31,303 --> 00:53:31,623
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not.

593
00:53:31,743 --> 00:53:38,309
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a recognition of... emotional recognition of a symptom, of a problem.

594
00:53:38,409 --> 00:53:44,634
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why anxiety pretty much is a sense of urgency that the Buddha would inspire in monks, the ones they got negligent.

595
00:53:44,894 --> 00:53:48,077
[SPEAKER_01]: So... and that's pretty much what you need to do first.

596
00:53:48,217 --> 00:53:49,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't try to get rid of it.

597
00:53:50,517 --> 00:53:55,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't try to find the answers for it, but surprise, endure it.

598
00:53:55,260 --> 00:54:05,705
[SPEAKER_01]: If you wonder how to say, like, you... you can't find your anxiety anymore, or it's not coming up, it's either... it's only for two reasons.

599
00:54:05,785 --> 00:54:08,186
[SPEAKER_01]: Either you became an arahant, or you became complacent.

600
00:54:10,208 --> 00:54:12,189
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're an arahant, no problem, you got your answer.

601
00:54:12,949 --> 00:54:20,353
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are complacent, all you need to do, and the anxiety will come back in no time, is tighten your sensory strength.

602
00:54:22,243 --> 00:54:23,984
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you'll see, it will be there.

603
00:54:24,264 --> 00:54:25,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Fear of death will be there.

604
00:54:25,364 --> 00:54:28,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Just by saying hello to sensuality, the first thing that comes knocking is fear of death.

605
00:54:30,406 --> 00:54:33,247
[SPEAKER_01]: And fear of dying, and dying your loved ones, or whatever else.

606
00:54:33,407 --> 00:54:34,907
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why people immediately run back.

607
00:54:34,927 --> 00:54:35,848
[SPEAKER_01]: They feel justified.

608
00:54:36,608 --> 00:54:37,128
[SPEAKER_01]: But, eh...

609
00:54:37,528 --> 00:54:41,970
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether it's a monk or a layperson, if you don't have anxiety, it means you don't have enough sense restraint.

610
00:54:43,270 --> 00:54:51,813
[SPEAKER_01]: And just think about it, I mean... Take an average layman and tell him, how about you abstain from sexual intercourse for the rest of your life, as a demand.

611
00:54:55,056 --> 00:54:58,518
[SPEAKER_01]: If it were to be taken seriously, every single person would freak out at that thought.

612
00:54:58,858 --> 00:55:04,981
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be this intense arising of pain and fear, at the thought of never experiencing that sensual pleasure ever again.

613
00:55:05,742 --> 00:55:10,664
[SPEAKER_01]: And within no time, they'll be full of anxiety, and the mind would be just swinging with the moods left and right.

614
00:55:11,785 --> 00:55:12,966
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and same for the monks.

615
00:55:13,006 --> 00:55:15,167
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is no anxiety, if there is no sense of urgency,

616
00:55:15,992 --> 00:55:20,393
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, oh, you are... you're getting complacent in your environment, in your setups or whatever else.

617
00:55:20,433 --> 00:55:26,874
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all you need to do is just start saying no to your eyes, ears, nose, mouth, touches and so on.

618
00:55:27,154 --> 00:55:30,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Avoid the comfort and so on and anxiety will be back.

619
00:55:30,235 --> 00:55:33,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Fear of death will be back, because it just reveals the situation you're still in.

620
00:55:34,676 --> 00:55:35,796
[SPEAKER_01]: You are not free from death.

621
00:55:42,037 --> 00:55:44,498
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you want to practice with anxiety and cultivate it,

622
00:55:45,490 --> 00:55:46,811
[SPEAKER_01]: The basis of virtue is necessary.

623
00:55:48,833 --> 00:55:53,316
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like an indicator of progression in training.

624
00:55:53,376 --> 00:55:53,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

625
00:55:53,976 --> 00:55:56,178
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not... Anxiety is not an indicator.

626
00:55:56,198 --> 00:55:57,719
[SPEAKER_01]: It's how much you resist it or not.

627
00:55:57,859 --> 00:55:59,360
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the indicator of your progression.

628
00:56:00,581 --> 00:56:10,948
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're not resisting it, not trying to justify it, get rid of it, and it's still there, then, yeah, then it's a good indicator that you're doing things rightly.

629
00:56:12,317 --> 00:56:14,619
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, how much anxiety you feel, that doesn't matter.

630
00:56:14,679 --> 00:56:27,311
[SPEAKER_01]: The same reason that people suffer so much and have fears, in the first reply, is because sensuality is the only means of escape people know, but it's the escape that makes the problem even worse.

631
00:56:27,411 --> 00:56:28,412
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not really an escape.

632
00:56:30,113 --> 00:56:32,634
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't try to not have anxiety.

633
00:56:32,714 --> 00:56:37,997
[SPEAKER_01]: You will, most certainly, if you start practicing sense restraint and keeping sīla and so on, and enduring solitude.

634
00:56:38,637 --> 00:56:43,619
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, ah, to train my mind to not resist it with aversion as something I must get rid of.

635
00:56:43,759 --> 00:56:45,200
[SPEAKER_01]: And then it will cease to be unpleasant.

636
00:56:46,741 --> 00:56:48,702
[SPEAKER_01]: It's only unpleasant, pretty much,

637
00:56:49,995 --> 00:56:55,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything that... any suffering, it's suffering because you are averse towards it, not because it's unpleasant in itself.

638
00:56:55,881 --> 00:56:57,523
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the truth of the First Noble Truth.

639
00:56:58,124 --> 00:57:07,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Your craving is the root of every suffering, even in those extreme circumstances where, I don't know, highway robbers attack a monkey and started hacking him with machetes and so on.

640
00:57:08,014 --> 00:57:16,058
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha said, if he has any resistance towards that emotionally, he's not... he hasn't fulfilled my teaching, because that's never where the suffering is rooted.

641
00:57:16,078 --> 00:57:21,081
[SPEAKER_01]: The extreme discomfort is not the root of dukkha, it's your aversion towards it, every single time, no exception.

642
00:57:22,302 --> 00:57:26,884
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anxiety is a problem, because people are averse towards it, not because it's a problem in itself.

643
00:57:27,164 --> 00:57:31,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, fear is a problem, it's because people are averse towards it, not because it's a problem in itself.

644
00:57:32,147 --> 00:57:36,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Fear may be just telling you, oh, this is a dangerous situation for my body, for my health, or for something.

645
00:57:37,657 --> 00:57:39,920
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's you being averse emotionally towards it.

646
00:57:39,960 --> 00:57:42,222
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you suffer on account of all of those mental states.

647
00:57:45,085 --> 00:57:45,246
[SPEAKER_03]: Sure.

648
00:57:48,263 --> 00:58:01,888
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I have to say that I didn't realize the necessary connection between increasing sense-restraint and increasing anxiety.

649
00:58:02,189 --> 00:58:16,274
[SPEAKER_03]: So, like, I know, let's say, if you adopt another sīla or additional sīlas, let's say, if you already attend to five or eight

650
00:58:17,955 --> 00:58:25,320
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, I know, you either see that you can do it or you see that you cannot do it.

651
00:58:25,460 --> 00:58:34,047
[SPEAKER_03]: And how do you mean that adding some additional sīlas is going to increase your anxiety?

652
00:58:34,547 --> 00:58:45,595
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I said, you will not be experiencing anxiety if you're either fully free from sensuality and almost an arahant, say, or because you're still using means of escape.

653
00:58:47,382 --> 00:58:48,163
[SPEAKER_01]: through sensuality.

654
00:58:48,943 --> 00:58:50,585
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why sensuality... that's what I mean.

655
00:58:50,625 --> 00:58:54,929
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not... sensuality is not so intoxicating for people just because it's pleasant.

656
00:58:55,049 --> 00:59:03,236
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because it's the only form of, like, approximation of an escape that they would know from the unpleasantness of existence in itself.

657
00:59:04,197 --> 00:59:06,739
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, as I said, even when people don't necessarily

658
00:59:08,938 --> 00:59:11,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Engage with coarse sensuality or real will.

659
00:59:12,080 --> 00:59:18,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Just having things to do, needing things to do, depending on activity of the senses, is still your means of escape.

660
00:59:18,524 --> 00:59:19,925
[SPEAKER_01]: So, start restraining that.

661
00:59:21,346 --> 00:59:32,013
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will feel anxious, because you are refusing to engage with the only direction of escaping the unpleasant pressure of unresolved existence of yourself.

662
00:59:32,174 --> 00:59:35,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you resolved it, you would be an arahant and there would be no pressure for you.

663
00:59:37,213 --> 00:59:39,254
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean by sensory strength.

664
00:59:39,274 --> 00:59:43,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, precepts, sure, that's fine, but now, ask yourself.

665
00:59:43,635 --> 00:59:44,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I'm doing...

666
00:59:44,495 --> 00:59:45,295
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm reading this book.

667
00:59:45,935 --> 00:59:47,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I self-aware while I'm doing that?

668
00:59:47,556 --> 00:59:48,136
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, not really.

669
00:59:48,156 --> 00:59:49,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, then I'll stop reading it.

670
00:59:49,416 --> 00:59:52,377
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just endure solitude and boredom and see where that takes me.

671
00:59:52,537 --> 00:59:56,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it will take you straight to anxiety, every single time, unless you have uprooted it.

672
00:59:57,218 --> 00:59:59,879
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why people are terrified of boredom, having nothing to do.

673
01:00:02,150 --> 01:00:09,778
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's pretty much, like, say, if you were to put an arahant in a cell without anything, he would not lose his mind, because there is no mind to be lost, in that sense.

674
01:00:10,279 --> 01:00:13,142
[SPEAKER_01]: He has dealt with it beforehand, so there's nothing for him to lose there.

675
01:00:13,722 --> 01:00:15,725
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing for him to resist there.

676
01:00:15,765 --> 01:00:22,472
[SPEAKER_01]: If you... if you lock him in a little box, he will not suffer, he will not experience terror or anxiety, because, basically, he's already abandoned this life.

677
01:00:26,607 --> 01:00:28,087
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean by increasing sīla.

678
01:00:28,127 --> 01:00:33,349
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't mean, like, add some random observances or precepts, mechanically, as a sense of duty.

679
01:00:34,069 --> 01:00:34,229
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

680
01:00:34,429 --> 01:00:41,091
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, find then other things you do within your good sīla and virtue, and see why you're doing them, and restrain them.

681
01:00:42,071 --> 01:00:44,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Wind yourself up a bit more, and you're gonna feel anxiety.

682
01:00:48,433 --> 01:00:48,993
[SPEAKER_01]: If you need to.

683
01:00:49,433 --> 01:00:51,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, don't do it if you're already full of anxiety.

684
01:00:52,965 --> 01:00:55,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can't have too much of a sense of urgency, as the Buddha would say.

685
01:00:55,967 --> 01:01:01,432
[SPEAKER_01]: It's only when you get... Oh, am I actually, you know, am I feeling the urgency of practice?

686
01:01:01,452 --> 01:01:01,952
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not sure.

687
01:01:01,992 --> 01:01:03,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, maybe I should restrain myself.

688
01:01:03,353 --> 01:01:06,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe today I will not see, talk to anyone.

689
01:01:06,256 --> 01:01:07,336
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not leave this room.

690
01:01:07,397 --> 01:01:09,118
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not switch TV or phone on.

691
01:01:09,238 --> 01:01:13,541
[SPEAKER_01]: I will just sit alone with myself, which is neutral in itself, by the way.

692
01:01:13,561 --> 01:01:16,384
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing bad, just sitting with yourself in a room with your own thoughts.

693
01:01:16,924 --> 01:01:17,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, nobody can do it.

694
01:01:18,045 --> 01:01:20,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, unless people who develop samādhi and then become arahants

695
01:01:21,888 --> 01:01:23,229
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize, okay, that's a challenge.

696
01:01:23,389 --> 01:01:26,411
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you go in that direction, as I said, anxiety is guaranteed.

697
01:01:26,591 --> 01:01:28,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Boredom leads to anxiety.

698
01:01:28,232 --> 01:01:29,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Anxiety leads to dread.

699
01:01:33,236 --> 01:01:41,101
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you recognize that you cannot deal with it overnight, but you certainly don't adopt the view that it should not be dealt with or you can avoid dealing with it.

700
01:01:41,401 --> 01:01:45,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's where arahantship is.

701
01:01:45,364 --> 01:01:46,144
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the Ajahn Chah?

702
01:01:46,184 --> 01:01:47,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Die before you die.

703
01:01:47,545 --> 01:01:49,847
[SPEAKER_01]: So, abandon this life before it abandons you.

704
01:01:54,917 --> 01:02:00,064
[SPEAKER_01]: And you abandon it by completely removing any desire towards it, any lust, any aversion and so on.

705
01:02:01,186 --> 01:02:02,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Any unwholesome mental state.

706
01:02:06,553 --> 01:02:11,300
[SPEAKER_06]: I have here one more written question from Raymond.

707
01:02:13,034 --> 01:02:16,695
[SPEAKER_06]: Maybe you already answered to that question, but are you ready to maybe have something to add?

708
01:02:17,776 --> 01:02:20,316
[SPEAKER_06]: How Buddhism understands self or ego?

709
01:02:21,057 --> 01:02:24,678
[SPEAKER_06]: So, is really this identity, self, the biggest enemy?

710
01:02:25,338 --> 01:02:27,679
[SPEAKER_06]: And then, how to treat or limit it?

711
01:02:27,719 --> 01:02:31,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, when people talk abstractly about it, it misses the point.

712
01:02:31,920 --> 01:02:33,220
[SPEAKER_01]: That's even when they ask the Buddha.

713
01:02:33,240 --> 01:02:38,802
[SPEAKER_01]: So, Master Gautama says there is no self, and he wouldn't answer it, because it's already wrong assumption.

714
01:02:40,924 --> 01:02:47,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Practically speaking, how you want to go about it is self, identity, ego, whatever you want to call it, it's a symptom of a problem.

715
01:02:49,009 --> 01:02:49,929
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a problem in itself.

716
01:02:52,371 --> 01:02:54,252
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you deal with the symptom of a problem?

717
01:02:54,332 --> 01:02:56,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you recognize what it is a symptom of.

718
01:02:56,793 --> 01:02:57,694
[SPEAKER_01]: And the suttas tell us.

719
01:02:58,174 --> 01:03:00,496
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a symptom of greed, aversion, delusion.

720
01:03:01,036 --> 01:03:03,217
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a symptom of not understanding the nature of existence.

721
01:03:03,257 --> 01:03:04,438
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a symptom of ownership.

722
01:03:06,206 --> 01:03:21,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Owning things, possessions, acquisitions, as the Buddha talked about, that you acquired through sensuality and pursuit of pleasure, that you get through those acquisitions, that's why you have a functioning sense of self that endures and becomes the owner, the the appropriator, the controller, the master, and so on.

723
01:03:22,068 --> 01:03:23,369
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you curb it?

724
01:03:23,590 --> 01:03:24,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, sense restraint.

725
01:03:25,031 --> 01:03:26,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Start saying no to your desires.

726
01:03:27,694 --> 01:03:30,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Relinquish unnecessary acquisitions.

727
01:03:30,436 --> 01:03:31,658
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't seek to gain more.

728
01:03:31,738 --> 01:03:32,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Contemplate death.

729
01:03:33,039 --> 01:03:37,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Contemplate losing things that are dear to you.

730
01:03:37,363 --> 01:03:38,885
[SPEAKER_01]: And you are curbing your sense of self.

731
01:03:38,985 --> 01:03:39,986
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's going to feel like dying.

732
01:03:40,066 --> 01:03:41,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, that self is dying.

733
01:03:42,920 --> 01:03:48,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Your identity is ceasing, and that's literally gonna feel like death, because that's what death is, cessation of identity.

734
01:03:49,045 --> 01:03:53,389
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Arahant cannot die, because he has already killed his identity, in that sense.

735
01:03:54,770 --> 01:03:56,051
[SPEAKER_01]: So death doesn't apply to him.

736
01:03:56,151 --> 01:04:07,600
[SPEAKER_01]: And even when the suttas talk about it, they say such and such person dies, such and such person dies, but when Arahant dies, they doesn't use the word die, it says aggregates break apart, because death doesn't apply to him.

737
01:04:08,281 --> 01:04:10,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Death applies when there is still lingering identity.

738
01:04:13,595 --> 01:04:30,318
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if I understand you, my name is Raymond, if I understand you very well, so, if I accept death like a normal state of my life, then I will be free of any illusions and... You can't accept it as a matter of choice, though.

739
01:04:30,918 --> 01:04:38,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as I said, the underlying problem of you not... the reason why you don't accept death, why you... why you fear death, that's it, that's already a form of non-acceptance.

740
01:04:40,122 --> 01:04:48,349
[SPEAKER_01]: is because you're not free from sensual desires, you're not free from aversion, you're not free from ignoring your own self, distracting yourself from yourself.

741
01:04:49,249 --> 01:04:52,532
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason why you don't accept death is not because you decided to not accept it.

742
01:04:52,792 --> 01:05:04,402
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can decide to accept it, but unless you uproot the underlying tendencies of lust, aversion, distraction, that feed the actual non-acceptance of it, you will not be free from it.

743
01:05:05,202 --> 01:05:15,805
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's good to accept death, but make sure you don't fall into a trap of, like, that psychologization, as I say, whereby, yes, I accept death, I'm at peace with it, and then continue with your desires and aversions.

744
01:05:16,605 --> 01:05:18,825
[SPEAKER_01]: That's actually, if you look closely, contradiction in terms.

745
01:05:19,005 --> 01:05:20,366
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just on the level of belief.

746
01:05:20,586 --> 01:05:26,967
[SPEAKER_01]: You believe that you accepted death, but you'll be surprised when the actual death comes, you realize how little that acceptance will matter.

747
01:05:27,527 --> 01:05:30,308
[SPEAKER_01]: It only mattered while you were alive, believing that you accepted it.

748
01:05:31,558 --> 01:05:34,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't need to decide to accept death or to decide to not accept death.

749
01:05:34,999 --> 01:05:46,364
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start diminishing your desires, practice sense restraint, and endure not acting out of craving towards your feelings, you are actually freeing yourself from death.

750
01:05:46,804 --> 01:05:47,965
[SPEAKER_01]: However, you know...

751
01:05:48,864 --> 01:05:51,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Small steps those might be, you are still going in that direction.

752
01:05:52,026 --> 01:06:05,052
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why some people in the suttas, when they never encountered the Buddha, but they were practicing on their own, and obviously were doing it more or less rightly, when they came across the Buddha, one sentence made them an arahant, fully enlightened, just hearing one instruction from the Buddha.

753
01:06:05,453 --> 01:06:10,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they actually did all that work of removing the underlying tendencies, because of which they were not enlightened.

754
01:06:11,396 --> 01:06:15,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Then the Buddha just set the pieces right, the pieces that they built and cultivated correctly.

755
01:06:17,191 --> 01:06:23,973
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't just accept it without having had that basis developed and cultivated thoroughly and sufficiently.

756
01:06:27,995 --> 01:06:28,355
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you.

757
01:06:28,375 --> 01:06:29,195
[SPEAKER_05]: Very good answer.

758
01:06:29,855 --> 01:06:32,016
[SPEAKER_05]: Can I put another question?

759
01:06:32,036 --> 01:06:39,379
[SPEAKER_05]: For example, I am 52 years old and all my life I have built a house of cards.

760
01:06:40,199 --> 01:06:46,321
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's very hard to me to destroy this house of cards.

761
01:06:48,277 --> 01:07:09,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so there's been like... it's... you will obviously have, you know, fear of death, recognition that it's... it's coming closer, but don't... you... you can think about it or not, it's up to you, but recognize that dealing with that problem, with that actual fear, is in controlling your other behavior towards life.

762
01:07:10,074 --> 01:07:12,315
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, how will I address with this now?

763
01:07:12,536 --> 01:07:15,138
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's what we meant, practice before it's too late.

764
01:07:16,227 --> 01:07:17,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now you still can.

765
01:07:17,327 --> 01:07:18,948
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter if you're 53 or 63.

766
01:07:18,968 --> 01:07:21,589
[SPEAKER_01]: You still have functioning senses.

767
01:07:21,869 --> 01:07:28,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Your faculties of making choices and being transparent in regard to your motivation about those are still there.

768
01:07:29,531 --> 01:07:41,515
[SPEAKER_01]: So, use that to start diminishing lust, acting out of lust, acting out of craving, acting out of aversion, enduring it on that emotional level and not giving in, keeping the precepts.

769
01:07:42,035 --> 01:07:44,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty much what the Buddha advised that king when he said

770
01:07:45,400 --> 01:07:57,515
[SPEAKER_01]: What would you do if there is a man from the east, west, north or south, four men come and tell you there are four great mountains coming your way and those mountains are crushing everything in their way?

771
01:07:58,437 --> 01:07:59,017
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no escape.

772
01:07:59,778 --> 01:08:02,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Every direction you try to run, you just run towards one of the mountains.

773
01:08:03,078 --> 01:08:04,720
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, well, what is there to do, Lord?

774
01:08:04,880 --> 01:08:11,629
[SPEAKER_01]: I would then be restrained, practice dhamma, make merit, keep the virtue, you know, that's pretty much the only left thing to do, because you can't outrun it.

775
01:08:12,189 --> 01:08:14,793
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says, well, so I'm telling you, there are four mountains crushing you.

776
01:08:14,813 --> 01:08:17,095
[SPEAKER_01]: It's old age and death coming your way.

777
01:08:17,876 --> 01:08:20,420
[SPEAKER_01]: So you better do what you just said you would do in that instance.

778
01:08:21,559 --> 01:08:22,200
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

779
01:08:22,240 --> 01:08:38,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you think about the mountains and death and how close you are, the way to deal with it is to start being mindful, sense restraint on the level of your day-to-day behavior and choices you make in between those reflections.

780
01:08:38,650 --> 01:08:41,392
[SPEAKER_03]: I have one question regarding monastic discipline.

781
01:08:42,015 --> 01:08:57,080
[SPEAKER_03]: So, as far as we know, some rules in Pāṭimokkha were caused just by, well, just by the opinions how things should be at the times of the Buddha, for instance, like not cutting trees and things like this.

782
01:08:57,720 --> 01:09:04,003
[SPEAKER_03]: So, why is it important nowadays to attend to these rules and still keep them?

783
01:09:04,323 --> 01:09:07,424
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that was the... it was actually that happened right after the Buddha died.

784
01:09:08,833 --> 01:09:14,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Ananda said that... oh, the Lord said we can abandon all these minor rules.

785
01:09:15,457 --> 01:09:19,659
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the Arahant said, the Council said, well, which one were the minor rules according to the Lord?

786
01:09:20,420 --> 01:09:22,421
[SPEAKER_01]: And Venerable Ānda said, well, I forgot to ask that.

787
01:09:23,758 --> 01:09:36,149
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they could have speculated and had a pretty good guess what the minor rule was, but then they said, well, if we do that, the first thing people will say, look, their head teacher died, and the first thing they do is start reducing the amount of rules.

788
01:09:36,970 --> 01:09:39,392
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let's keep all of them, since it makes no difference.

789
01:09:39,672 --> 01:09:45,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the rules that you have to keep, that have nothing to do with the dhamma, are not in the way of your practicing the dhamma.

790
01:09:50,442 --> 01:09:55,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Many of those rules, as well, were often qualified in themselves.

791
01:09:55,529 --> 01:09:58,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, this is an offense, unless it's done for this reason.

792
01:09:59,474 --> 01:10:01,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, in case of this, you can do this.

793
01:10:01,657 --> 01:10:03,199
[SPEAKER_01]: But if that's not the case, you can't do that.

794
01:10:03,985 --> 01:10:05,927
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, they're quite flexible in themselves.

795
01:10:06,087 --> 01:10:09,289
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there isn't really any of those rules that would be in the way if you're practicing correctly.

796
01:10:09,830 --> 01:10:10,930
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the reason why it's kept.

797
01:10:11,211 --> 01:10:20,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Just because Ananda forgot to ask and it would have looked bad upon the order and upon the value of dhamma for those who haven't understood it but might have had chance to understand it.

798
01:10:20,578 --> 01:10:22,239
[SPEAKER_01]: They say, look, I don't want to listen to them.

799
01:10:23,120 --> 01:10:25,402
[SPEAKER_01]: The teacher died and they were already disrespecting him.

800
01:10:26,322 --> 01:10:27,643
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it was for benefit of others.

801
01:10:27,683 --> 01:10:29,605
[SPEAKER_01]: They still adhere to the rules and we do so.

802
01:10:35,497 --> 01:10:44,200
[SPEAKER_06]: Any more questions?

803
01:10:44,240 --> 01:10:44,860
[SPEAKER_06]: Second time?

804
01:10:47,261 --> 01:10:47,881
[SPEAKER_06]: Third time?

805
01:10:48,522 --> 01:10:52,423
[SPEAKER_06]: Thank you very much for your time and for very good answers.

806
01:10:53,764 --> 01:10:54,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, have a nice day then.

